n":231},"Supply Chain Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsupply-chain-security","02.platform\u002F14.supply-chain-security","i-lucide-package-check",{"title":233,"path":234,"stem":235,"icon":236},"Enterprise License","\u002Fplatform\u002Fenterprise-license","02.platform\u002F15.enterprise-license","i-lucide-badge-check",{"id":238,"title":33,"body":239,"description":526,"extension":527,"links":528,"meta":529,"navigation":530,"path":34,"seo":531,"stem":35,"__hash__":532},"docs\u002F01.apps\u002F03.application-structure\u002F03.artifact-types.md",{"type":240,"value":241,"toc":516},"minimark",[242,251,255,258,263,287,292,303,306,310,317,321,329,333,348,351,355,358,362,370,374,382,386,389,393,398,402,407,411,414,418,423,427,435,439,442,513],[243,244,245,246,250],"p",{},"Every project in a Kinotic application contributes one or more ",[247,248,249],"strong",{},"artifacts",". An artifact is a typed output that the platform knows how to build, deploy, and manage. This page describes each artifact type and when to use it.",[252,253,59],"h2",{"id":254},"persistence",[243,256,257],{},"The most common starting point. A persistence artifact defines your domain models (entities) and gets auto-generated CRUD services in return.",[243,259,260],{},[247,261,262],{},"What you provide:",[264,265,266],"ul",{},[267,268,269,270,274,275,278,279,282,283,286],"li",{},"Entity classes decorated with ",[271,272,273],"code",{},"@Entity"," and field-level annotations like ",[271,276,277],{},"@NotNull",", ",[271,280,281],{},"@Precision",", and ",[271,284,285],{},"@AutoGeneratedId",".",[243,288,289],{},[247,290,291],{},"What the platform provides:",[264,293,294,297,300],{},[267,295,296],{},"Full CRUD operations (save, find, delete) with pagination and sorting.",[267,298,299],{},"Named queries derived from method naming conventions.",[267,301,302],{},"Automatic storage provisioning and indexing.",[243,304,305],{},"Persistence is typically the first artifact you create because it establishes the data model that the rest of the application builds on.",[252,307,309],{"id":308},"microservice","Microservice",[243,311,312,313,286],{},"A microservice artifact is long-running code that executes in a pod or VM. It publishes services that other parts of the application can discover and call through ",[314,315,316],"a",{"href":52},"service proxies",[243,318,319],{},[247,320,262],{},[264,322,323,326],{},[267,324,325],{},"TypeScript classes that implement your business logic.",[267,327,328],{},"Service interfaces that you want to expose to the rest of the application.",[243,330,331],{},[247,332,291],{},[264,334,335,338,345],{},[267,336,337],{},"Automatic service registration in the Service Directory.",[267,339,340,341,344],{},"Service discovery and zone-based routing so other projects can call your service through a ",[314,342,343],{"href":52},"service proxy"," with a simple function call.",[267,346,347],{},"Lifecycle management, scaling, and health monitoring.",[243,349,350],{},"Use a microservice when your logic goes beyond simple CRUD -- orchestrating across multiple entities, calling external APIs, or running long-lived workflows.",[252,352,354],{"id":353},"batch-job","Batch Job",[243,356,357],{},"A batch job artifact is code that runs on a schedule. It is ideal for periodic tasks like data aggregation, report generation, cleanup routines, or syncing with external systems.",[243,359,360],{},[247,361,262],{},[264,363,364,367],{},[267,365,366],{},"TypeScript code that performs the job.",[267,368,369],{},"A schedule definition (cron expression or interval).",[243,371,372],{},[247,373,291],{},[264,375,376,379],{},[267,377,378],{},"Scheduled execution with retry and failure handling.",[267,380,381],{},"Logging and observability for each run.",[252,383,385],{"id":384},"ui-component","UI Component",[243,387,388],{},"A UI component artifact produces reusable building blocks that can be shared across multiple frontends within the application. Think of it as an internal component library.",[243,390,391],{},[247,392,262],{},[264,394,395],{},[267,396,397],{},"Reusable UI components packaged for consumption by frontend projects.",[243,399,400],{},[247,401,291],{},[264,403,404],{},[267,405,406],{},"Versioning and distribution within the application.",[252,408,410],{"id":409},"frontend","Frontend",[243,412,413],{},"A frontend artifact is a complete user interface that gets deployed as a static site. It consumes services published by other projects in the application.",[243,415,416],{},[247,417,262],{},[264,419,420],{},[267,421,422],{},"A web application (built with any framework) that compiles to static assets.",[243,424,425],{},[247,426,291],{},[264,428,429,432],{},[267,430,431],{},"Static hosting with CDN distribution.",[267,433,434],{},"Automatic connection to the application's services through the Kinotic client libraries.",[252,436,438],{"id":437},"combining-artifact-types","Combining Artifact Types",[243,440,441],{},"Most real-world applications combine several artifact types. A typical setup might look like this:",[443,444,445,461],"table",{},[446,447,448],"thead",{},[449,450,451,455,458],"tr",{},[452,453,454],"th",{},"Project",[452,456,457],{},"Artifact Type",[452,459,460],{},"Role",[462,463,464,477,489,501],"tbody",{},[449,465,466,472,474],{},[467,468,469],"td",{},[271,470,471],{},"data",[467,473,59],{},[467,475,476],{},"Defines entities and provides auto-generated CRUD services",[449,478,479,484,486],{},[467,480,481],{},[271,482,483],{},"services",[467,485,309],{},[467,487,488],{},"Implements business rules that orchestrate across entities",[449,490,491,496,498],{},[467,492,493],{},[271,494,495],{},"jobs",[467,497,354],{},[467,499,500],{},"Runs nightly data aggregation",[449,502,503,508,510],{},[467,504,505],{},[271,506,507],{},"web",[467,509,410],{},[467,511,512],{},"The main user interface",[243,514,515],{},"Each project is independently developed and versioned, but they all deploy as part of the same application and communicate through the Service Directory.",{"title":517,"searchDepth":518,"depth":518,"links":519},"",2,[520,521,522,523,524,525],{"id":254,"depth":518,"text":59},{"id":308,"depth":518,"text":309},{"id":353,"depth":518,"text":354},{"id":384,"depth":518,"text":385},{"id":409,"depth":518,"text":410},{"id":437,"depth":518,"text":438},"Learn about the different artifact types a Kinotic project can contribute -- from persistence and microservices to frontends.","md",null,{},true,{"title":33,"description":526},"ks4mH_HWM4uaDA9Vwx-yIdl366mKkKWDw9PgfC_UDoU",[534,536],{"title":29,"path":30,"stem":31,"description":535,"children":-1},"Learn how to configure Kinotic applications and the projects that compose them.",{"title":25,"path":44,"stem":45,"description":537,"children":-1},"How services work in Kinotic and the communication patterns they support.",1792928917440]